    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d24">

                <pb facs="0119" n="25" sameAs="frus:frus1887/pg25"/>

                <head>No. 24.<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Bayard to Mr. Tree</hi>.</head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Department of State</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1886-12-03"><hi rendition="#italic">December</hi> 3,
                        1886</date>.</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 66.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I inclose a copy of a letter from Capt. J.
                    F. Burke, commanding the Gate City Guard, of Atlanta, Ga.</p>
                <p>This volunteer organization, which is highly commended by his excellency the
                    governor of Georgia, is one of the foremost in the country for drill and
                    discipline.</p>
                <p>As you will see by the inclosure it is the deeire of the organization which is
                    about visiting Europe to wear their uniforms and to carry their arms when on
                    parade.</p>
                <pb facs="0120" n="26" xml:id="pg26"/>
                <p>Please obtain early information whether the Government to which you are
                    accredited will give the necessary permission, and under what, if any,
                    conditions, and apprise me so that I can notify Governor Gordon in season.</p>
                <p>I am, etc.,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">T. F. Bayard.</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
